  <section id="access">
    <h2>Finding legal access</h2>
    <p>
      The hardest part of river kayaking in much of the UK is not the paddling; it is working out where you may legally get on and off the water. In England and Wales, the public does not have a general right to launch from any riverbank. Banks are often private, and rights of navigation vary from river to river. Scotland is different: the Land Reform (Scotland) Act 2003 gives a right of responsible access to most inland water, though you still need to respect landowners and other users.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start by finding out who manages your river. Our <a href="/rivers/">river guides</a> name the main regulator for each river, and the Canal &amp; River Trust, Environment Agency and Paddle UK all publish access information. For licence and membership questions, read <a href="/blog/do-i-need-a-license-to-paddleboard-on-a-river/">our access and licence guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="launch">
    <h2>Where people launch</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Canoe and rowing clubs.</strong> Many have a slipway and welcome visitors who ask in advance. Some charge a small fee.</li>
      <li><strong>Public slipways and waterside parks.</strong> Local authority riverside parks and signed public slipways are the most straightforward options. Check for signs that restrict launching.</li>
      <li><strong>Managed navigations.</strong> The Canal &amp; River Trust and similar bodies maintain launch points and portages on some navigations. A waterways licence or Paddle UK membership is normally needed on those waters.</li>
      <li><strong>Pubs and campsites on the river.</strong> Some allow launching for customers, but only with the owner's permission. Never assume.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Even where you can launch, think about your exit: a long trip means arranging a second access point and a way back to your car. Tidal rivers are different again, as the tide changes the current and how much water there is to launch from.
    </p>
  </section>

